              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Figure Q21–2 Somite formation in zebrafish embryos (Problem 21–15). (A) Wild-type embryos with normal somites. (B) Somite formation in embryos lacking Delta. The bracket indicates normal-looking somites where they initially form. (C) Somite formation in embryos lacking Delta but receiving a pulse of Delta expression at the time indicated by the right-hand bracket. (D) Interactions among components of the oscillatory clock in adjacent cells. (Adapted from C. Soza-Ried et al., Development 141:1780–1788, 2014. With permission from the Company of Biologists.)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.524,
            0.099,
            0.923,
            0.131
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What is it about the presence of Delta that keeps adjacent cells oscillating in synchrony?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.524,
            0.137,
            0.924,
            0.311
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "21–16 The extracellular protein factor Decapentaplegic (Dpp) is critical for proper wing development in Drosophila ("
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure Q21–3A",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "). It is normally expressed in a narrow stripe in the middle of the wing, along the anterior– posterior boundary. Flies that are defective for Dpp form stunted “wings” ("
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure Q21–3B",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "). If an additional copy of the gene is placed under control of a promoter that is active in the anterior part of the wing or in the posterior part of the wing, a large mass of wing tissue composed of normal-looking cells is produced at the site of Dpp expression ("
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure Q21–3C and D",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "). Does Dpp stimulate cell division, cell growth, or both? How can you tell?"
            }
          ]
        },
